# Level 1 → Level 2

## Concept
This level tests how to read a file with an unusual name — specifically 
a file named `-` (just a dash). Normally `cat -` would be interpreted by 
the shell as "read from standard input" rather than "read the file named 
dash," because a leading `-` is treated as a command flag. The challenge 
is knowing how to get around that.

## Commands used
cat < -

## Why it works
The `<` operator redirects input from a file directly into the command, 
rather than passing the filename as an argument. Since `cat` normally 
reads from standard input anyway, feeding it the file's contents via `<` 
sidesteps the shell trying to interpret `-` as a flag. The file's contents 
(the password) get printed to the terminal as a result.
